{"from": "text:mishnah-bm-7-1", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-20-01-early-hiring", "grade": "B", "note": "Mishnah Bava Metzia 7:1 rules on employers who tell hired labourers to 'rise exceptionally early', against local custom: the first-light hiring of field labourers, with customary working hours, was an institution the rabbis regulated (context: the mishna is later compilation, so C not B). Redacted c. 200 CE, so it may not describe the practice of the 30s CE exactly. Regraded C to B per the architect's Mishnah ruling (2026-11-15): the Mishnah names the same practice the claim states."}
{"from": "site:hurvat-itri", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-20-01-vineyards-winepress", "grade": "C", "note": "Hurvat Itri, a Second Temple-period Jewish village in the Judean Lowlands at its peak in the early first century CE, was excavated with numerous rock-cut wine presses: viticulture with presses was the working setting of a Judean landowner's vineyard. Regraded C by architect review: shows Judean viticulture of the period; the parable has no specific setting to confirm."}
{"from": "observation:grape-fig-cultivation-levant", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-20-01-vineyards-winepress", "grade": "C", "note": "Wine 'has been produced in the Land of Israel since biblical times' and was 'exported to Rome during the Roman period'; grapes are a staple of ancient Israelite agriculture (reuse of the ch 07 observation)."}
{"from": "artifact:archelaus-coins", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-20-01-vineyards-winepress", "grade": "C", "note": "Herod Archelaus's Judean prutot (4 BCE-6 CE) depict a bunch of grapes: grape-growing was the standard iconography of the Judean economy in the period (reuse from ch 02)."}
{"from": "artifact:denarius", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-20-02-denarius-a-day", "grade": "C", "note": "The denarius was the standard Roman silver coin from c. 211 BCE into the 3rd century CE, the everyday wage coin of the eastern provinces in the first century; the gospels' use of it as a day's wage is documented by source:wikipedia-denarius on the claim (C, not B: the coin is real, the day-wage rate is documented but not on the coin)."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-pesachim-5-1", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-20-03-04-daylight-hours", "grade": "C", "note": "Mishnah Pesachim 5:1 fixes the daily offering 'at eight and a half hours of the day, which is two and a half hours after midday': the Temple day was reckoned in twelve numbered daylight hours counted from sunrise, so the third, sixth, ninth and eleventh hours were mid-morning, noon, mid-afternoon and late afternoon (mishna compiled c. 200 CE, so C)."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-bm-9-11", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-20-08-pay-same-day", "grade": "B", "note": "Mishnah Bava Metzia 9:11: 'A day laborer collects his wages from his employer all night following his work shift' - a day labourer must be paid when the working day ends (mishna compiled c. 200 CE, so C). Regraded C to B per the architect's Mishnah ruling (2026-11-15): the Mishnah names the same practice the claim states."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-bm-9-12", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-20-08-pay-same-day", "grade": "B", "note": "Mishnah Bava Metzia 9:12 makes wages 'subject to the prohibition of: On the same day you shall give him his wages (Deuteronomy 24:15), and... The wages of a hired laborer shall not remain with you all night until the morning (Leviticus 19:13)' - same-day payment as a binding rule. Redacted c. 200 CE, so it may not describe the practice of the 30s CE exactly. Regraded C to B per the architect's Mishnah ruling (2026-11-15): the Mishnah names the same practice the claim states."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-17-death", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-20-18-condemned-by-chief-priests", "grade": "C", "note": "Context: Josephus shows Herod appointing and deposing high priests in his last days, confirming the priestly establishment in Jerusalem to whom the Son of Man is said to be delivered (reuse of the ch 02 grade-C context, as in ch 16)."}
{"from": "artifact:yehohanan-heel-bone", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-20-19-mocked-scourged-crucified", "grade": "C", "note": "The excavated (1968, Giv'at ha-Mivtar) heel bone of Yehohanan, crucified in the 1st century CE, with a nail driven through it: Roman crucifixion was practiced in Judaea in the period - context for the predicted fate, not the specific event the verse predicts (ch 16 keeps B for the 16:24 custom claim, which is the custom itself)."}
{"from": "text:josephus-war-2-varus", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-20-19-mocked-scourged-crucified", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus (War 2.5.2) records that Varus crucified 'about two thousand' Jews in 4 BCE: mass Roman crucifixion in Judaea a generation earlier - context for the predicted handing over to the Gentiles, not the specific event (ch 16 keeps B for the 16:24 custom claim)."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-17-cruelty", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-20-25-rulers-lord-it-over", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus records Herod ordering 'that one out of every family should be slain, although they had done nothing that was unjust', to make the nation mourn at his death: a client king lording it over his subjects with absolute and arbitrary power (the 'rulers of the Gentiles' of 20:25, C)."}
{"from": "artifact:delphi-manumission-inscriptions", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-20-28-ransom-for-many", "grade": "C", "note": "About a thousand Delphi inscriptions of 200 BCE-100 AD record slaves freed 'through fictitious sales to the god Apollo' for a price in silver: paying a sum to release a slave is abundantly documented in exactly the Greek-speaking world of the lytron word. The fetched translations do not themselves quote the word lytron; its meaning 'price of release, ransom' is documented by source:wiktionary-lytron on the claim."}
{"from": "site:herodian-jericho-palaces", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-20-29-out-of-jericho", "grade": "B", "note": "The excavated Hasmonean and Herodian winter palaces at Tulul Abu el-'Alayiq, with the garden city around them, show that first-century Jericho was a wealthy royal centre on the road up to Jerusalem - the Herodian city Matthew names, distinct from the Old Testament tell."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-17-death", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-20-29-out-of-jericho", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus places Herod's last days and the public announcement of his death at Jericho ('the king's death was made public... in the amphitheater at Jericho'): a royal residence city at the end of the first century BCE."}
{"from": "text:psalms-of-solomon-17", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-20-30-31-cry-son-of-david", "grade": "C", "note": "'Raise up unto them their king, the son of David' - a first-century BCE Jewish psalm expecting a Davidic messiah: the title the blind men cry from the roadside (reuse from ch 12)."}
{"from": "observation:jerusalem-jericho-ascent", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-20-17-18-going-up-jerusalem", "grade": "C", "note": "Jerusalem's Old City sits at about 760 m in the Judaean Mountains while Jericho lies at -258 m in the rift valley: travelling from Jericho (or the Jordan valley) to Jerusalem is literally an ascent of roughly 1,000 m, the geography behind 'going up to Jerusalem'."}
